WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.785
Next to the print shop's front counter is a cloakroom.

2
00:00:05.785 --> 00:00:08.298
You hand in your coat, and you get a ticket.

3
00:00:08.298 --> 00:00:12.183
Later you hand back the ticket, and the attendant fetches your coat.

4
00:00:12.183 --> 00:00:14.353
You never walk into the back yourself.

5
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:18.226
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:21.893
In Kotlin you can write: var status: String by something.

7
00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:23.887
What does by actually do there?

8
00:00:23.887 --> 00:00:28.776
Is there some magic, some reflection at run time, looking things up by name?

9
00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:31.198
Let's start without by.

10
00:00:31.198 --> 00:00:34.497
Say every change to an order's status must be logged.

11
00:00:34.497 --> 00:00:40.347
And you want the logging written once, in a small helper class you can reuse for any property.

12
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:45.022
By hand, the property forwards its getter and setter to the helper.

13
00:00:45.022 --> 00:00:50.542
In Java, the helper is an ordinary Box object, and getStatus and setStatus call it.

14
00:00:50.542 --> 00:00:56.395
Kotlin by hand looks the same: a private helper field, and a get and a set that call it.

15
00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:03.028
::status is a reference to the property itself, a KProperty object that knows the property's name.

16
00:01:03.028 --> 00:01:08.603
So the helper can log the word status, without a string that could drift out of date.

17
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:09.372
It works.

18
00:01:09.372 --> 00:01:14.517
But that's three lines of plumbing for every property, and the plumbing never changes.

19
00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:17.928
Ten logged properties, thirty lines that say nothing new.

20
00:01:17.928 --> 00:01:22.236
Wouldn't it be nice to just say: this property goes through that helper?

21
00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:23.769
That's all by does.

22
00:01:23.769 --> 00:01:30.350
Write var status: String by Audited("NEW"), and the compiler writes the same forwarding for you.

23
00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.447
Same for copies.

24
00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:37.390
The helper needs only two functions with fixed names, getValue and setValue.

25
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.818
getValue gets the object that owns the property, and the KProperty.

26
00:01:41.818 --> 00:01:44.000
setValue also gets the new value.

27
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.842
Audited prints each change, then stores it.

28
00:01:46.842 --> 00:01:48.891
A val would need only getValue.

29
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.466
Back to the cloakroom.

30
00:01:50.466 --> 00:01:52.204
The property is your ticket.

31
00:01:52.204 --> 00:01:56.426
Reading the property is handing in the ticket to get your coat back.

32
00:01:56.426 --> 00:01:58.723
Assigning to it is handing in a coat.

33
00:01:58.723 --> 00:02:04.870
And the attendant is the helper object: it holds the coat, and it may write each swap in a logbook.

34
00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.167
That helper has a name: the delegate.

35
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:13.377
So what does the compiler build inside Order, with its two delegated properties?

36
00:02:13.377 --> 00:02:14.894
How many hidden fields?

37
00:02:14.894 --> 00:02:15.949
Pause and guess.

38
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:23.894
The demo sets the status and the copies, then lists the fields by reflection.

39
00:02:23.894 --> 00:02:25.315
Here's the real output.

40
00:02:25.315 --> 00:02:27.603
Each change went through the logbook.

41
00:02:27.603 --> 00:02:29.272
And there are three fields.

42
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:34.027
One hidden field per property: status$delegate and copies$delegate.

43
00:02:34.027 --> 00:02:36.324
Each holds its own Audited helper.

44
00:02:36.324 --> 00:02:44.836
And one static array per class, $$delegatedProperties, with a KProperty for each property, made once when the class is loaded.

45
00:02:44.836 --> 00:02:50.647
That's the only change from our hand-written version: no fresh ::status on every call.

46
00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:53.511
Now the getter, in bytecode.

47
00:02:53.511 --> 00:02:56.428
getStatus loads the status$delegate field.

48
00:02:56.428 --> 00:02:59.276
It loads the array, and takes entry zero.

49
00:02:59.276 --> 00:03:01.637
It calls getValue on the delegate.

50
00:03:01.637 --> 00:03:03.998
And it casts the result to String.

51
00:03:03.998 --> 00:03:05.873
A hidden field, and a call.

52
00:03:05.873 --> 00:03:06.568
That's it.

53
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:08.546
So, is it magic?

54
00:03:08.546 --> 00:03:08.742
No.

55
00:03:08.742 --> 00:03:11.422
Nothing is looked up by name at run time.

56
00:03:11.422 --> 00:03:15.082
If by had a longer name, it would be: please forward to.

57
00:03:15.082 --> 00:03:20.376
The compiler writes the plumbing you wrote by hand, and then gets out of the way.

58
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.864
Java sees none of this.

59
00:03:22.864 --> 00:03:26.266
It sees getStatus and setStatus, ordinary methods.

60
00:03:26.266 --> 00:03:31.979
The demo's Java twin calls setStatus, and the change still goes through the logbook.

61
00:03:31.979 --> 00:03:34.020
The delegate field is private.

62
00:03:34.233 --> 00:03:38.291
That's handy for libraries that work through getters and setters.

63
00:03:38.291 --> 00:03:44.721
It's also a warning: a setter that logs, or checks, looks exactly like a plain setter from the outside.

64
00:03:45.633 --> 00:03:48.528
And here's where the cloakroom picture breaks.

65
00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:51.611
A real cloakroom has one attendant for everybody.

66
00:03:51.611 --> 00:04:00.483
Here, every ticket usually comes with an attendant of its own: a hidden field in every Order, and usually a new delegate object to put in it.

67
00:04:00.483 --> 00:04:04.007
Make many orders, and each one hires its own attendants.

68
00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:09.215
So use by when the same get and set logic repeats across properties.

69
00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:12.489
Remember the hidden field it adds to every instance.

70
00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:16.708
And remember that from the outside, it looks like a plain property.

71
00:04:16.933 --> 00:04:20.841
The delegate you'll use most often isn't one you write yourself.

72
00:04:20.841 --> 00:04:23.040
It's lazy, and it comes with a bill.

73
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.505
That's the next section.

74
00:04:25.433 --> 00:04:26.792
So, what does by do?

75
00:04:26.792 --> 00:04:28.219
It hands in a ticket.

76
00:04:28.219 --> 00:04:34.605
A hidden field holds the attendant, and the getter and setter just call getValue and setValue.

77
00:04:34.833 --> 00:04:35.742
Quick check.

78
00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:37.635
You add var note: String?

79
00:04:37.635 --> 00:04:39.604
by Audited(null) to Order.

80
00:04:39.604 --> 00:04:41.345
What new fields appear?

81
00:04:44.533 --> 00:04:46.452
One new field, note$delegate.

82
00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:50.157
And a third entry in the same array, not a second array.
